15 0 obj
<< /A 51 0 R /Alt (Flowchart illustrating a deep learning pipeline for stain bias suppression in pathology images, beginning with a patch-partitioned histology slide, followed by multiple Swin Transformer stages, feature extraction, a stain decomposition adapter, gating mechanism, weighted image outputs, a spatial transformer classifier head, and final output.) /K 0 /P 8 0 R /Pg 35 0 R /S /Figure /T () >>
endobj
16 0 obj
<< /A 52 0 R /Alt (Flowchart diagram illustrating a dual-branch neural network architecture for histopathology image analysis, starting with feature extraction, separating into stain-related and morphology-dominant branches to obtain color and structure features, followed by attention-based fusion and computation of output.) /K 0 /P 8 0 R /Pg 36 0 R /S /Figure /T () >>
endobj
17 0 obj
<< /A 53 0 R /Alt (Flowchart illustrating a graph neural network architecture with weighted features as input, progressing through graph-transformer, graph structure estimation, dynamic graph convolution, fusion, and output layers. Patch features are visualized as nodes, with adjacent features in red and non-adjacent in blue, and processed in sequential sub-graphs. Legend at right defines node color meanings.) /K 0 /P 8 0 R /Pg 37 0 R /S /Figure /T () >>
endobj
18 0 obj
<< /A 54 0 R /Alt (Side-by-side line graphs compare training loss and validation loss curves across epochs for Dataset one and Dataset two. Both show decreases in loss over time, with validation and training lines closely aligned, indicating consistent model performance and convergence in both datasets.) /K 0 /P 8 0 R /Pg 42 0 R /S /Figure /T () >>
endobj
19 0 obj
<< /A 55 0 R /Alt (Four confusion matrices compare classification performance between a baseline and a proposed method across two datasets, with axes labeled Normal and OSCC for true and predicted labels. The matrices for \215Ours\216 show increased true positive and true negative values and decreased misclassification rates compared to \215Baseline\216 for both datasets, indicating improved classification accuracy. Color bars represent value intensities.) /K 1 /P 8 0 R /Pg 43 0 R /S /Figure /T () >>
endobj
20 0 obj
<< /A 56 0 R /Alt (Two t-SNE scatter plots visualize test set data, comparing \215Normal \(True\)\216 in blue, \215OSCC \(True\)\216 in orange, and misclassified samples outlined in black. Left plot shows some separation between groups, while the right plot shows more overlap and greater misclassification, as indicated by increased black-outlined points.) /K 0 /P 8 0 R /Pg 43 0 R /S /Figure /T () >>
endobj
21 0 obj
<< /A 57 0 R /Alt (Side-by-side comparison of medical histology slides from Dataset 1 on the left and Dataset 2 on the right, each row showing original tissue images, corresponding heatmaps with red and yellow highlights, and merged overlays.) /K 0 /P 8 0 R /Pg 44 0 R /S /Figure /T () >>
endobj
22 0 obj
<< /A 58 0 R /Alt (Grid of ten line charts with error bars displays accuracy, precision, recall, F1-score, and AUC for two datasets as learning rate increases. All metrics show decreasing scores for higher learning rates.) /K 0 /P 8 0 R /Pg 45 0 R /S /Figure /T () >>
endobj
23 0 obj
<< /A 59 0 R /Alt (Grouped bar chart showing performance metrics versus training data volume for two datasets. Metrics include accuracy, precision, recall, F1-score, and AUC, measured at 40, 60, 80, and 100 percent training data. Dataset 1 generally outperforms Dataset 2 across all metrics except precision, where Dataset 2 achieves higher values. Error bars show standard deviation. Color and pattern distinguish datasets, with Dataset 1 in blue diagonal stripes and Dataset 2 in orange diagonal stripes. Chart legend is present at the bottom right.) /K 0 /P 8 0 R /Pg 47 0 R /S /Figure /T () >>
endobj
